{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that 'rationality is simply the commitment to critical scrutiny' fails to address the specific pragmatic dilemma posed in the problem: why prefer Theory A (highly corroborated) over Theory B (untested but unfalsified) if both are equally 'critical'? Popper rejects induction, so past survival cannot logically imply future reliability. The target asserts this preference is 'responsible' but provides no non-inductive mechanism to justify why responsibility tracks with corroboration rather than novelty or simplicity. By defining rationality as merely 'commitment to scrutiny,' the target sidesteps the core charge of smuggling induction back in under the guise of 'responsibility,' effectively restating the preference without defending its logical basis on Popper's own anti-inductivist terms.","premise":"Popper explicitly rejects the principle that past success implies future probability; therefore, any preference for the past-successful theory requires a justification that does not rely on the predictive value of past performance.","successor_question":"If rationality is only 'commitment to scrutiny,' what specific non-inductive rule dictates that scrutinizing the well-corroborated theory is more rational than scrutinizing a new, bold conjecture?","target":"ff2a75bf005f0cb8989909c75fad17b8ce1fba227b79d8fd38cfaebf9c094b2f"}]}